### What issue are you seeing?

On macOS 15.7.4 with Codex CLI 0.157.1, a fresh interactive codex session selected my custom permission profile with network.enabled = true, but curl -I https://example.com failed with curl: (6) Could not resolve host. Inside that shell, scutil --dns reported “No DNS configuration available.” DNS worked outside Codex, and the same request succeeded through codex exec and codex sandbox -P with the profile. Enabling [features] network_proxy = true made curl work in a fresh interactive session. The Mac uses Tailscale DNS.


### What steps can reproduce the bug?

Environment: macOS 15.7.4. codex --version reported 0.157.1; the failed thread metadata and Codex doctor reported app-server 0.158.0. Tailscale DNS was active.

Configuration at the time of failure (network_proxy was unset):

default_permissions = "workspace-net"

[permissions.workspace-net]
extends = ":workspace"

[permissions.workspace-net.network]
enabled = true

[permissions.workspace-net.network.domains]
"*" = "allow"

1. Start a fresh interactive session with codex -C /path/to/trusted/project.

2. Ask Codex to run curl -I --max-time 10 https://example.com in its shell.

3. Observe curl: (6) Could not resolve host: example.com (exit code 6), although the thread’s network permission is enabled.

4. In the same environment, scutil --dns reported “No DNS configuration available.” DNS and curl worked outside the interactive Codex shell. A codex sandbox -P workspace-net test and a codex exec test also reached the site.

5. Adding [features] network_proxy = true and starting another fresh interactive session made curl return HTTP 200.

Failed thread IDs: 01a0e787-ddaf-7d31-969a-7ae00f898d16 (standalone curl failure) and 01a0e7a7-87f4-78c2-bf12-27d92beda394 (scutil --dns and curl failure).

### What is the expected behavior?

network-enabled interactive sessions can resolve hosts, or Codex explains that its network proxy is required.

### Additional information

With Tailscale connected, an interactive Codex shell without network_proxy could not see DNS configuration and curl failed. After disconnecting Tailscale, a fresh interactive session with network_proxy=false and no HTTPS_PROXY returned HTTP 200. With Tailscale connected, a host-level codex -c features.network_proxy=false doctor --json run reported overallStatus: "ok"
